  "narrations": {
    "who-gets-blamed--narration-0": {
      "id": "who-gets-blamed--narration-0",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When a microservice system fails, root cause analysis models read its metrics, logs and traces to name the service that started it."
        }
      ]
    },
    "who-gets-blamed--narration-1": {
      "id": "who-gets-blamed--narration-1",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do they find the cause, or just the loudest service?"
        }
      ]
    },
    "the-shortcut--narration-0": {
      "id": "the-shortcut--narration-0",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "On public benchmarks, a crude rule does as well as they do: blame whichever service raises the most alerts."
        }
      ]
    },
    "the-shortcut--narration-1": {
      "id": "the-shortcut--narration-1",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It works because in 86 percent of benchmark cases the fault is loud only where it was injected, or barely shows at all. Following a failure as it spreads is rarely tested."
        }
      ]
    },
    "faults-that-travel--narration-0": {
      "id": "faults-that-travel--narration-0",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So we built one where failures do spread. On a fifty-service ticketing system under shifting traffic, we injected thirty-one kinds of fault."
        }
      ]
    },
    "faults-that-travel--narration-1": {
      "id": "faults-that-travel--narration-1",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We kept only the failures users could feel: 1,430."
        }
      ]
    },
    "the-scores-fall--narration-0": {
      "id": "the-scores-fall--narration-0",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Eleven state-of-the-art models, on average, name the right service first just 21 percent of the time, and the alert rule falls apart too."
        }
      ]
    },
    "the-scores-fall--narration-1": {
      "id": "the-scores-fall--narration-1",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Progress in root cause analysis has to be measured where failures travel."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
